description:
Written on reverse of card: &quot;Michigan Ave., Lansing, Mich.&quot;

Image appears to have been taken from the intersection of Michigan and Washington Avenues in downtown Lansing. Shows a horse at left foreground, a streetcar and buggies, several electricity poles along the street, and buildings along East Michigan Avenue looking east. A sign for Stahl's Hardware (213-215 North Washington Avenue) is visible on one of the buildings.

Photographer and date not identified.

&quot;The 'beaus' of Belleville&quot; Two men sitting outside a building with sign reading &quot;83rd Can. Res. Squn. R.F.C&quot;
&quot;THE Squadron office&quot;
&quot;Two good buses, 83 C.T.S.&quot; Two aircraft beside a hanger, tail numbers C638 and C556

One of a series of photographic copies of pages of an album of Royal Flying Corps/Royal Air Force photographs taken at Camp Mohawk near Deseronto, Ontario, and Benbrook in Texas. 
The photographs of the album pages were found in Texas and scanned by Jay Foersterling, who donated the scanned images to the Community Archives in July 2021.

&quot;Picture looking from [Q].V. Park / B toward the City&quot;
Queen Victoria park in Belleville, Ontario, looking toward City Hall.  Three people are walking on the path between the river and the inner harbour.  Also visible is a bridge across the river and boats in the inner harbour.  

One of a series of photographs in the John Fairbairn Anderson Collection, early 1900s.

&quot;Birds eye view from mountain, Wooler, Ont.&quot;
Postcard of Wooler, Ontario, with Wooler Methodist (later United) Church in the centre of the image.

Donated by Norma Lentz in November 2017.

&quot;Moire River, Belleville, Ontario.-26.&quot;
This photo is taken from the area near the Upper Bridge, looking south.  City Hall and the Lower Bridge are visible in the background
Tinted postcard of the Moira River taken from the west bank looking south from approximately Moira Street, Belleville, Ontario.

&quot;INTERIOR OF ABU SCARGA CHURCH, OLD CAIRO, JAN. 20, 1910&quot;
Group of people inside a stone building [Abu Serga church, Cairo, Egypt] with columns and screens.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;THE BEACH AT TYNEMOUTH, ENG. 3-28-1910&quot;
A crowded Long Sands beach at Tynemouth, England. The building on the left is the Tynemouth Palace (later the Tynemouth Plaza, destroyed by fire in 1996).

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;COLOMBO HARBOR&quot;
View of small boats and waterfront buildings in the harbour at Colombo, Sri Lanka (then Ceylon).

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;LEAVING SYDNEY FOR AUCKLAND, NOV. 11TH, 1908.&quot;
The quayside in Sydney, Australia, with people on the dock and the Union Steam Ship Company of New Zealand building in the background. On the hill in the distance is the Model Lodging House. 

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;MAORI CHURCH, THAMES, N.Z. 3-1-09&quot;
Two members of the Kilties band in a building with elaborate carvings and two statues. [This is the <a href="http://thamesnz-genealogy.blogspot.ca/2015/02/thames-nz-house-of-hotunui.html" rel="nofollow">House of Hotunui</a>.]

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;OUR CHRISTCHURCH CARTERS &quot;
Four men (one in Kilties costume) standing with a horse and cart outside a hotel on a city street in Christchurch, New Zealand, with a sign for J. Rhodes in the background. J. Rhodes was a gentlemen's outfitters, described in advertisements as being at 187 High Street, &quot;opposite the clock tower&quot;, which was at the intersection of High Street, Manchester Street and Lichfield Street at this date.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;THE AVON, CHRISTCHURCH, NEW ZEALAND, 12-11-08&quot;
A man and woman in a rowboat passing under a wrought iron bridge with an older man standing and some cyclists on the bridge.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;THE AVON, CHRISTCHURCH, NEW ZEALAND, 12-11-08&quot;
Two rowboats on the Avon river lined with willow trees.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;THE AVON, CHRISTCHURCH, NEW ZEALAND, 12-11-08&quot;
A man and woman standing near a garden in a parkland beside a river.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;A FEW OF THE SIGHTS AT WAKAREWAREWA, MARCH 3RD, 1909&quot;
&quot;IN THE HOT BATH&quot;
A group of people standing outside a building. There is a white man, woman and child along with two Maori adults and a group of children apparently in a hot bath.  Photograph taken in Whakarewarewa, New Zealand.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;JUST BEFORE SAILING VICTORIA B.C. JULY 17TH 1908&quot;
Woman [Lena Power] standing in front of a building with arched windows.

One of a series of photographs from an album of the Kilties' world tour.

&quot;Belleville Ont. taken from an aeroplane
Copyright Canada 1919 by Bishop Barker Co. Limited Toronto&quot;

Aerial photograph of Belleville, Ontario, looking across Moira Street to the Moira River, the Upper Bridge and Front, Pinnacle and Church Streets.  the spires of Tabernacle Episcopal Methodist Church, St. Michael's Roman Catholic Church and St. Andrew's Presbyterian Church are visible in the background

&quot;Bank of Commerce &amp; Baxters Hotel - Gow Ganda&quot;

View of the Canadian Bank of Commerce building in Gowganda, taken from the lake. The bank was built of logs and there is a canoe on the bank's veranda. The building behind the bank has the words SILVER..NO THEATRE just visible upon it.

One of a set of photographs from a trip to &quot;Gow Ganda&quot;, May 19 - June 28, 1909.

&quot;Steamer Brockville S. S. Picnic&quot;

The <em>Brockville</em>, with many people on board. This photograph was taken on 9 July 1908, possibly at Massassauga Point, Prince Edward County, Ontario, as part of a Sunday School excursion (presumably from the Presbyterian Church of the Redeemer in Deseronto, Ontario, of which Harold McMurrich Rathbun, the photographer, was a member).

Compare with <a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/deserontoarchives/3778009054/">HMR2-06-34</a> (a better image, taken slightly closer to the ship).

&quot;Group at S. S. Picnic - Massassauga&quot;

Adults outside the ice-cream pavilion at Massassauga Point, Prince Edward County, Ontari, part of a Sunday School excursion (presumably from the Presbyterian Church of the Redeemer in Deseronto, Ontario, of which Harold McMurrich Rathbun, the photographer, was a member).

This photograph was taken on 9 July 1908.

&quot;84 C.T.S. Just a few of them&quot; four aircraft lined up outside two hangars.

&quot;84 C.T.S. C541? 30th May 1917 Capt. V. Castle dives into a hangar Cadet Fraser burnt to death and Capt Castle slightly injured. The hangar in flames&quot;

Photos from an album that belonged to Christopher Paulus Devos, a sergeant in the Royal Flying Corps who was an instructor for 84 Canadian Training Squadron, based at Camp Mohawk, near Deseronto, Ontario.

&quot;Sgt Major J. F. Chambers i/c 84 C.T.S&quot;

&quot;Sgts De Feu &amp; Hellewell&quot;

&quot;Vernon Castle in centre giving advice&quot; 

Photos from an album that belonged to Christopher Paulus Devos, a sergeant in the Royal Flying Corps who was an instructor for 84 Canadian Training Squadron, based at Camp Mohawk, near Deseronto, Ontario.

&quot;84 C.T.S. C-586, Air Mechanic Wilson nearly killed&quot;

This accident took place on <a href="http://www.rafmuseumstoryvault.org.uk/archive/?7000289484" rel="nofollow">October 9th, 1917</a>.

Photo from an album that belonged to Christopher Paulus Devos, a sergeant in the Royal Flying Corps who was an instructor for 84 Canadian Training Squadron, based at Camp Mohawk, near Deseronto, Ontario.

&quot;All 'O.K.'&quot;

&quot;Nose-diving&quot;

&quot;'Climbing' at Rathbun Camp&quot;

&quot;86 C.T.S. 'Some Swimmers'&quot;

&quot;86 C.T.S. again&quot;

Showing a number of crashes, two in the Bay of Quinte (one with Foresters' Island and some buildings on it visible in the background). Aircraft numbers are C610, C275 and C271.

Photos from an album that belonged to Christopher Paulus Devos, a sergeant in the Royal Flying Corps who was an instructor for 84 Canadian Training Squadron, based at Camp Mohawk, near Deseronto, Ontario.

Newscopy: &quot;A six-minute ride in a 30-inch wide elevator brought a News photographer to the top of the Marin tower of the Golden Gate Bridge for this view of the approach to the span, through the Marin Hills, via the Waldo tunnel.&quot;

Click <a href="http://sflib1.sfpl.org:82/record=b1025632~S0" rel="nofollow">here </a> for further information or a high resolution copy of this image.

Rights: Permission to use this image commercially must be obtained from the San Francisco History Center, San Francisco Public Library.  <a href="http://www.sfpl.org/permissions" rel="nofollow">www.sfpl.org/permissions</a>  When using this image please credit SAN FRANCISCO HISTORY CENTER, SAN FRANCISCO PUBLIC LIBRARY.

Newscopy: &quot;Way, way up was News Photographer Bob Klein when he took these pictures of workmen painting the North Tower of the Golden Gate Bridge. At right, Klein's feet, resting precariously some 700 feet above the water, appear to be of interest to painters William Gavre, top, and Hale Sharrett, below.&quot;

Click <a href="http://sflib1.sfpl.org:82/record=b1035700~S0" rel="nofollow">here </a> for further information or a high resolution copy of this image.

Rights: Permission to use this image commercially must be obtained from the San Francisco History Center, San Francisco Public Library.  <a href="http://www.sfpl.org/permissions" rel="nofollow">www.sfpl.org/permissions</a>  When using this image please credit SAN FRANCISCO HISTORY CENTER, SAN FRANCISCO PUBLIC LIBRARY.

&quot;The audience which saw the opening performance of &quot;The Squall&quot; at the early evening session at the Theatre Royal yesterday laughed quite often, but the play was not a comedy.&quot; 

&quot;THE SQUALL&quot; (1944, September 1). The Sydney Morning Herald (NSW : 1842 - 1954), p. 4. Retrieved April 19, 2021, from <a href="http://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article17919257" rel="noreferrer nofollow">nla.gov.au/nla.news-article17919257</a>

The Squall, theatrical production, Theatre Royal, Sydney, 29/8/1944, by Sam Hood, State Library of New South Wales, Home and Away - 30819,  <a href="https://archival.sl.nsw.gov.au/Details/archive/110027973" rel="noreferrer nofollow">archival.sl.nsw.gov.au/Details/archive/110027973</a>

Shows pump house and lagoon and park road coming down from upper left, around to parking area and combination building on right, near river. 

Specific date: 08/17/1937. 

Artist/designer: Hammond. Copy, black on tan paper, and colored (in crayon?) green, brown, yellow, blue, red. With contour lines and key map. LN: 36.0&quot; x 23.875&quot;

Plan of building, including terrace, pavilion, shower rooms, concession service, kitchen, and store rooms. Floor slab arrangements. 

Specific date: 04/29/1935. 

Artist: Smith, Olin. Pencil and red, green, and brown ink on wax tracing paper. With notes and bill of material. LN: 34.625&quot; x HT: 23.375&quot;

Plan and typical wall section AA of Refectory building. With trees and road and San Marcos River. 

Specific date: 03/06/1935

Artist: Smith, Olin. Pencil (and red and green and blue crayon on back) and black ink on tracing paper. LN: 33.625&quot; x HT: 22.75&quot;
